 ("h2", "The Beginnings: Harappan Architecture"),
 ("ul", ["The earliest known tradition of **urban planning** in the subcontinent: planned cities, drainage, water management and civic organisation.", "Most cities used **bricks**, as they lay in alluvial plains where clay is plentiful.", "**Mohenjo-daro** (Sindh): wide streets and narrow lanes, almost every home had a bathing area, used water left through drains.", "**Dholavira** (Rann of Kachchh): used **stone**, had a fortified **Castle** area, a sentinel room at the gate and large reservoirs.", "Harappan **wells** used trapezoid-shaped bricks that lock together, so the walls did not collapse inward.", "Larger houses had a **central courtyard**, a design still used 4,500 years later."]),
 ("h2", "Stupas and Rock-Cut Caves"),
 ("table", ["Type", "Key points", "Examples"], [["Stupa", "Dome-shaped, holds relics of the Buddha or monks; railings (vedika) and gateways (toranas); Buddha shown through symbols such as footprints and the wheel of dharma", "Sanchi (Ashoka, Madhya Pradesh), Bharhut (donor inscriptions)"], ["Rock-cut caves", "Carved into rock for worship, residence or assembly; durable and efficient", "Udayagiri–Khandagiri (Odisha, 2nd century BCE, Jain monks, Kharavela inscription); Ajanta and Ellora (Maharashtra)"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Pradakshina** means walking around a sacred structure in a clockwise direction. It began at stupas and continued in later temples."),
 ("ul", ["**Kailasha Temple, Ellora** is a monolithic temple: thousands of tonnes of rock were chipped away to leave it standing.", "**Ellora cave 10** has an arched roof copying timber beams, a seated Buddha leaning on a stupa, and an aisle for pradakshina."]),
 ("h3", "Don't miss out"),
 ("ul", ["**Sharada Peetha** (Kashmir): famed centre of learning, linked to the Sharada script, flourished 6th–12th centuries; now in ruins.", "**Hidimba Devi Temple** (Manali): 16th century, dedicated to Hidimba from the Mahabharata.", "**Jageshwar group** (Uttarakhand): about 124 stone temples.", "**Golden Temple** (Punjab): Sikhs' holiest shrine, begun by Guru Ramdas, continued by Guru Arjan Dev in 1588."]),
 ("h2", "Water Structures"),
 ("ul", ["India's monsoon climate led to many ways of **harvesting, storing and sharing water**, from village ponds to grand works.", "**Shringaverpur** (Uttar Pradesh): linked reservoirs filled by a Ganga channel in flood season; early tanks let silt settle.", "**Stepwells (vavs or baoris)** of Gujarat and Rajasthan: **Rani ki Vav** has pillared levels and hundreds of statues; they also gave a cool meeting place.", "**Pushkarinis** are temple reservoirs for water harvesting and rituals, e.g. at Vijayanagara.", "Many are threatened today by pollution, waste and poor upkeep."]),
 ("h2", "Classical Temple Architecture"),
 ("p", "Classical temples began in the **Gupta era** and followed the rules of **shilpashastra** (treatises on arts and crafts). Temples were centres of worship, learning, art and economic life."),
 ("table", ["Part", "Meaning"], [["Gopuram / torana", "Ceremonial entrance"], ["Mandapa", "Pillared hall"], ["Garbhagriha", "Innermost chamber with the deity's image, facing east"], ["Shikhara / vimana", "Tower above the garbhagriha (north / south India)"], ["Prakara", "Enclosure wall of a large complex"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "A temple is planned as the body of **Purusha**, the cosmic being: the tower is the head and the platform the feet. Worship is a journey from the outer world to the inner shrine."),
 ("table", ["Temple", "Place and date", "Features"], [["Brihadishvara", "Thanjavur, built by Rajaraja Chola I around 1010", "Vimana over 60 m, big mandapas, Nandi from one stone, granite carvings"], ["Lingaraja", "Bhubaneswar, early 11th century", "Shiva shrine, three mandapas, sandstone and laterite, ornate carvings"], ["Dilwara", "Mount Abu, 11th–13th centuries, Solanki dynasty", "Five Jain temples in white marble, carved on every surface"], ["Bishnupur", "West Bengal, 17th–18th centuries", "Brick temples with terracotta panels of Radha, Krishna, Rama and daily life"]]),
 ("p", "From the 10th century temples grew into vast complexes. Their planning needed advanced geometry, as with Egypt's pyramids and Europe's Gothic cathedrals. None of these structures used cement mortar."),
 ("h2", "Mosques, Forts and Palaces"),
 ("ul", ["**Mosque**: prayer hall facing Mecca, domes, minarets, mihrab; large ones are called **Jama Masjid**.", "**Jama Masjid, Ahmedabad** (Ahmed Shah I, 15th century): yellow sandstone, jalis and temple-style pillars; shows **Indo-Islamic** blending with local Hindu and Jain artisans.", "**Jama Masjid, Delhi** (Shah Jahan, mid-17th century): India's largest mosque, red sandstone and white marble, two 40 m minarets, courtyard for 25,000 people.", "**Mehrangarh Fort** (Jodhpur): red sandstone from the mid-15th century; palace now a museum; Jaswant Thada is a cenotaph.", "**Agra Fort**: rebuilt by Akbar in red sandstone from Dholpur; Shah Jahan added marble palaces; fountains cooled it.", "**Orchha**: Bundela Rajput complex on the Betwa with Jahangir Mahal (Rajput–Mughal blend).", "**Mysuru Palace**: Indo-Saracenic, early 20th century, golden throne displayed at Dasara."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "A **cenotaph** remembers a person but is not a tomb, since the body is cremated elsewhere."),
 ("h2", "Tombs, Houses, Churches and Colonial Buildings"),
 ("ul", ["**Humayun's Tomb** (Delhi, 1558): commissioned by Bega Begum, Persian architects; symmetry, gardens, six-pointed star.", "**Taj Mahal**: Shah Jahan's tomb for Mumtaz Mahal; Persian charbagh garden, Arabic calligraphy and Indian chhatris and lotus designs; inlay of semi-precious stones.", "**Havelis** of Shekhawati (Rajasthan): central courtyard, frescoes; many now hotels or museums.", "**Chettinadu houses** (Tamil Nadu): merchant homes using Burmese teak and Italian marble; courtyards for rituals and drying papads.", "**Churches** in Kerala mix local styles, e.g. the stambha at Mulanthuruthy and the tiled roof at Kalloopara.", "**Indo-Saracenic** (British Raj): blends Islamic, Hindu and Gothic elements, as in the Madras High Court and Victoria Memorial."]),
